{
  // Layout of Black Ops Cold War's WEAPON asset.
  //
  // Every field carries whether its name is the engine's own or this project's. 'engine' means the
  // name is declared in a debug build of the neighbouring title, which shares this struct's
  // lineage. 'observed' means the engine declares nothing for it and the name here describes what
  // the field was seen to hold. An observed name is a label, not a fact, and should be treated as
  // one; an engine name is the game's own word for the field.
  //
  // Reference fields were confirmed by dereferencing them across every weapon and seeing where
  // they land, which is exact: an address either belongs to a loaded asset or it does not. Hash
  // fields were confirmed by resolving them to a name and reading what that name is.
  //
  // The numeric fields are named by the weapons that survive from an earlier title, where the
  // field names are known: a value carried over unchanged sits at some offset here, and an offset
  // holding the same field's value across many weapons is that field. Every weapon type that
  // title exports is a donor, since they all share the one struct.
  //
  // What is kept:
  //   - three or more adjacent offsets matching three or more fields adjacent in the earlier
  //     struct, in the same order, which is a whole group landing intact;
  //   - two adjacent offsets where at least 60 weapons agree on at least three distinct values and
  //     the name also appears in the field list a debug build of this lineage declares.
  //
  // Competing runs are settled by weight of evidence, longest and best attested first, and a run
  // that would take an offset already spoken for is dropped whole rather than fragmented. Claiming
  // in address order instead silently costs the stronger run its members: the sway group lost
  // three of its six that way, and two of the remaining three landed on worse offsets than the
  // data supports.
  //
  // 'observed' is how many of the loaded assets set the field. It is recomputed from the export
  // rather than written by hand, so a layout can be checked against the game instead of
  // trusted: a field that stops being set is a field that moved.
  //
  // 'agreed', where a field has one, is the older measure - how many of the shared weapons
  // agreed on that field when the name was placed. It is how well attested the name is, which
  // is a different question from how many assets carry a value, and worth keeping apart.
  //
  // 166 weapons are present in both titles.
  //
  // Offsets are only valid for the struct size recorded here.
  //
  // Audited afterwards, which is what settles whether a name sits on its own field or on a
  // neighbour that happens to carry similar numbers. For every offset the audit asks which
  // field fits it best, and for every field which offset fits it best. Being contradicted is
  // not itself wrong - a title re-tunes a weapon and the value legitimately differs - but
  // being contradicted more often than agreed while a distant offset carries the field better
  // is the mark of a misplaced name, and that is how a contiguous but poorly supported run
  // beats a scattered but well supported one.
  //
  // Fields failing that test were moved where the better offset was unclaimed and the two
  // chose each other, and dropped otherwise. A field flagged only because an adjacent sibling
  // fits marginally better is left alone: fields holding the same value as one another cannot
  // be separated by value at all, and their order within the run is what tells them apart.
  //
  // Two animation tables sit side by side, and they hold different things. The one at 0x4d0 is
  // 387 slots of viewmodel animation - what the player sees of the weapon in their own hands -
  // and the one at 0x4d8 is 330 slots of third person animation, what everyone else sees. Every
  // entry in each is named accordingly, which is what tells them apart.
  //
  // Both sizes were measured. Viewmodel entries stop at slot 386 and nothing lands in the fourteen
  // slots after it; third person entries stop at 329 with a long empty run beyond. The two tables
  // are usually allocated one after the other, so reading either without a size runs into its
  // neighbour and mixes the two.
  //
  // The third person table is built in blocks of thirty, one per posture, so slot n and slot n+30
  // are the same action standing, crouched and prone. The viewmodel table has no such stride: its
  // slots are distinct actions.
  //
  // The two animation tables are named on that basis. The engine declares one animation field,
  // szXAnims, and the earlier title in this lineage records viewmodel animations in it - its
  // fireAnim is vm_annihilator_fire - so the table of vm_ entries is szXAnims and carries the
  // engine's name. The table of third person entries beside it has no declared name at all, so
  // the name it carries here is only a description of its contents.

  "struct": "WeaponDef",
  "pool": 33,
  "size": 6424,
  // What the export gathers its fields under, in the order they are written.
  "groups": ["models", "animations"],

